I see something interesting in `ParseFlexBufferValue`:

```cpp
case kTokenFloatConstant: {
  double d;
  StringToNumber(attribute_.c_str(), &d);
  builder->Double(d);
  EXPECT(kTokenFloatConstant);
  break;
}
```

Here, `double d;` is declared uninitialized, and `StringToNumber` is called. If `StringToNumber` fails, `d` remains uninitialized (well, actually `StringToFloatImpl` sets `*val = 0` on failure, so it should be initialized). But wait - let me check `StringToFloatImpl` again:

```cpp
inline bool StringToFloatImpl(T *val, const char *const str) {
  FLATBUFFERS_ASSERT(str && val);
  auto end = str;
  strtoval_impl(val, str, const_cast<char **>(&end));
  auto done = (end != str) && (*end == '\0');
  if (!done) *val = 0;  // erase partial result
  return done;
}
```

This always sets `*val` on failure. So `d` should be initialized.

